pt plus the proposed tokens in one forward pass, computing logits for every position in that sequence. This lets it verify multiple draft tokens at once instead of generating them one by one.\n\nThe key trick is a rejection test that preserves the target model\u2019s normal output distribution. For each proposed token, the method compares the target probability with the draft probability. If the target model is at least as confident as the draft, the token is accepted. If not, the token is rejected and a new token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s probabilities while accounting for the draft\u2019s proposal. Because this correction is derived from the target and draft distributions, the final sequence has the same distribution as if the target model had decoded alone, assuming the same random seed and sampling parameters.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how many draft tokens are accepted before a rejection, the relative cost of the draft and target models, and the efficiency of the target\u2019s parallel verification. A good draft model that matches the target\u2019s preferences can accept long runs, while a poor one causes frequent rejections. The two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are a low draft acceptance rate and the overhead of running the draft model plus the target verification pass, especially when the target model is already fast or the batch size is large.\"\n\nCount words. Let's count manually.\nSpeculative1 decoding2 speeds3", "top2": [[[1596, -0.0022959820926189423], [760, -6.94197416305542]], [[1144, -1.78815535036847e-05], [4087, -11.446402549743652]], [[4087, -0.20071302354335785], [310, -1.7106082439422607]], [[1156, -0.007343667093664408], [303, -4.96311616897583]], [[579, -0.24660955369472504], [25, -1.7955483198165894]], [[1622, -0.008773254230618477], [3296, -5.053292751312256]], [[25, -0.33351489901542664], [13, -1.2894550561904907]], [[59987, -0.08994931727647781], [7860, -2